    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite is green: **45 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned owner identity so `customerCode` is now `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, dropping the per-city sequence numbers entirely.\n\n### The change\n\n**`AssignCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 rewrote the core logic:\n- **REGION** now comes from `Localities.region(postcode, city)` \u2014 postcode-range-first (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), falling back to the city table, matching the locality derivation.\n- **HASH8** is the first 8 upper-case hex characters of `SHA-256(normalizedTelephone + lastName)`.\n- Removed the old `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` format, the per-city counting, and the now-unneeded `OwnerRepository` dependency.\n\n### Why nothing else needed logic changes\n\nThe step runs after `normalizeTelephone`, so `owner.getTelephone()` is the stored E.164 value that the mapper returns unchanged \u2014 the hash matches the response telephone the tests recompute against. Everything downstream already keys off `owner.getCustomerCode()` generically, so it automatically inherits the new identity:\n- **membership number** (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`),\n- **check digit** (Luhn over the code's digits),\n- **create audit record** (logs the new `customerCode`, e.g. `NSW-8C073917`).\n\n### Doc updates (kept consistent, not behavioral)\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 `customerCode` and `membershipNumber` schema descriptions/examples.\n- Javadocs in `AssignCustomerCode`, `AssignMembershipNumber`, and `CustomerCodeCheckDigit`.\n\nAll checkpoint tests pinning the new contract (Cp09/14/16/21/31/32, plus locality Cp17/30 and identity Cp11/29) pass, and the complete suite is green.",
